{"cases":[{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"63f71ce7d4da20778df37cc61c493f02e48ba07f2b11c42f416ad196cd74288e"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues against the panel's superiority as a base-rate artifact but its own mechanism undercuts the argument: majority voting suppresses false positives only if individual errors are random, yet random errors would equally suppress false negatives, producing accuracy above base rate — a genuine gain by SRC_002's own logic, not mere compliance. More critically, the claim that a checklist with high sensitivity but low specificity induces uninformative individual judgments is self-defeating: high sensitivity means the checklist correctly identifies positives, so individual judgments that follow it are informative about positives by definition. The base-rate artifact requires low sensitivity (always guessing negative), contradicting the stated high-sensitivity premise.","counterexample":["Task: 95% negative cases, 5% positive","Checklist: high sensitivity (e.g., 90% of positives correctly flagged), low specificity (e.g., 50% of negatives correctly rejected)","Panel: each member votes independently using checklist, majority vote aggregated","Expected: false positives from low specificity are partly cancelled by majority voting (random errors), while true positives are consistently captured (high sensitivity), yielding accuracy above the 95% base rate — a genuine reliability gain, not a base-rate artifact"],"target":"e95cb4f1c4ca2abacfd3e1befbd2fc5ee9cd4867bfee105781b8883620e7a02a"}]}